 take the steps to come out of lockdown  to move on from the restrictions that  we've had to have over  the the last you know months most of the  last year and  we've we're in a much much better place  now we've had nearly 16 million people  vaccinated that's a huge deal we have an  enormous testing capacity as well  and we've learned a huge amount about  the virus we've got better treatments  for it as well  um so i see us being in a much better  place as we come through this but we  will of course hear more from the prime  minister on monday  yeah a huge deal and indeed a huge  achievement of course and  uh the roots out of all of this though  is is of course where the focus is and  specifics will come on monday but  i understand that but as you said we all  want this to be the last  national lockdown the signal from  central government has been that we want  to be  relaxing things on a national basis  some suggest you know in the papers this  morning that that if there were to be an  outbreak we could see a return to  localized  lockdown conditions what's your view in  there  well i'd say probably wait until next  week until  uh when the prime minister sets out that  road map for how we're going to come  through it  um but we have clearly learned a huge  amount we learned during the summer  about  how to manage localized outbreaks and  take that approach  um but i i don't really want to preempt  what we're going to hear next week  sure i thought i thought that might well  be the response um well let's let's talk  about schools are we of course  uh march the eighth big date from for  many many people  not least not least the parents in all  of this and again we know that testing  in schools is going to be  is going to be part of the way we move  out of all of this but we're now being  told that  we could be seeing teachers in secondary  schools administering  one set of tests and then the  responsibility being put on to the  parents  at least for for secondary school  students  well first i mean i like like many uh  thousands of parents i'm really looking  forward to when schools go back i've  got three children at home homeschooling  and  i think as all of us know who are doing  that it's not the easy things  thing to do and gives you even more  respect than you already had for  the teachers who usually uh teach your  children in fact have been doing an  amazing job providing online support  during  the pandemic um so you know we're  looking forward to  the plan is for schools to be able to  start going back on the 8th of march  and again next week more will be set out  about how the return to school is going  to work  i i understand that but one thing we can  say with certainty is you know from that  react study that's out at the moment  clearly some of the greatest  transmission that is taking place or the  highest amount of transmission that's  taking place is  is taking place in children it is taking  place in younger people  now that may well be a result of the  fact that they're not in school that  they're associating at home  but does that not give any pause for  concern does that give any pause ahead  of the decision to reopen schools on  march 8th  well i mean what has been clear is that  we will look at the data  and the reason why we have put off  saying what the roadmap is going to be  until next week was to make sure that we  had time  to look at the data to to know that we  have vaccinated so many people to be  able to look at the impact  of the national lockdown on rates to  also  see how that has enabled hospitals to be  in a better position to cope  and there's one reason why a level of  caution is being counseled  to to recognize the lockdown is going to  we're going to come out of it step by  step  because we still have over 20 000 people  in hospital  we've covered we still had over a  thousand new admissions on saturday for  example  so no this isn't over yet though we have  this fantastic situation we're in of  nearly 16 million people  vaccinated the vaccination program going  at such a tremendous pace and there's a  brilliant job being done by the nhs and  by volunteers some of them i've  met who are supporting the vaccine  program but we are going to have to take  the step by step but what we've always  said is we're determined to bring back  schools  as quickly as we can because we know  that  being out of school is not the good  thing for children's education it's so  much better for children to be in school  and that's really important that we try  and get that to happen i mean tell us a  little bit minister about the additional  funding  that's going to be made available for  for looking at long covered i mean  clearly it is a condition of concern for  for all of us  but particularly of course for those  people who've been working on the front  line and have contracted covert through  you know doing their job and looking  after the rest of us  oh you're absolutely right and i mean  what we're seeing is about one in ten  people  uh suffering from some form of long  covet so having  some symptoms that go on for three  months or more quite a range of symptoms  um but you know some are really making a  really tough to  live with and so we need to understand  more about this uh illness and how long  covered affects people and also how to  treat people and support people that's  one reason why today we're announcing  18 million pounds more going into  research  into long covered and exactly that how  we can help support people and treat  people and help people through it  i i'm part of the reason minister of  course that i mentioned those working on  the front lines  some really interesting data out today  about people applying to become  nurses nursing applications are in fact  up by a third  you know i've got to say slightly  surprising to me that in the middle of a  viral pandemic you know people  are wanting to go out there and help but  actually perhaps it'